--- Under an aliased import (`use math as m`), a call of the program's OWN
--- function named like one of math's (`rank`, `mean`) is refused, both modes:
---   index_finite_lists_of_positive_integers-1.co:21:1: 'rank' comes from math which was imported as 'm' — call m.rank[...]
---     21 │ log[rank[[1, 2]]]
--- The definition `fn rank` itself is accepted; only the calls are refused, and
--- the message names math's rank, which the program does not call.
--- Expected: 2. The spec (Aliased Imports) says an aliased import is
--- qualified-only — "nothing enters your bare namespace except the alias
--- itself" — and that "for the stdlib this doubles as the opt-out when you want
--- a name the module owns (`sum`, `mean`, `e`)". A variable so named works
--- (`sum = 0` beside `m.sum[xs]` runs); a function so named does not.
--- (Without any import of math, the same program prints 2 in both modes.)
--- Found writing Index finite lists of positive integers: a rank/unrank pair in
--- a program that needs math only for `m.seed[…]`.

use math as m

fn rank[xs]
    return len[xs]

log[rank[[1, 2]]]
